 >>
 >> At this point I can't boot the system with MILO. I'm trying to do it=20
with a
 >> command similar to the one I used to boot the 2.6.8-4 kernel:
 >>
 >> boot sdb6:vmlinuz-2.6.18-6-alpha-generic
 >> initrd=3Dinitrd.img-2.6.18-6-alpha-generic root=3D/deb/sdb7
 >>
 >> Disk is partitioned to the Sarge default structure.
 >> sdb5 is the space reserved for aboot (empty)
 >> sdb6 is /boot (ext2 - MILO is not able to read ext3)
 >> sdb7 is / (ext3)
 >> sdb8 is swap
 >>
 >> It seems MILO loads the image, but then fails to boot (it displays
 >> bootfile=3D<some odd characters>.
 >>
 >> Any hints how to start it?
 >>
 >
 > Do you have a compelling reason to use AlphaBIOS/MILO?
 >
 > Matt

This machine is dual-booting NT and Debian Sarge, so AlphaBIOS is the=20
only possibility. And the flash chip can either contain AlphaBIOS or=20
SRM. I know it's possible to start the machine with SRM on a floppy, but=20
this is what I'm trying to avoid. Since the 2.6.8 kernel booted, it=20
confirms that MILO is capable of booting newer kernels, but it seems it=20
fails to load the image properly to memory. I guess it might be possible=20
to generate the loaded image in some legacy format? I noticed that=20
during the kernel upgrade the imagerd is generate by some tool, so maybe=20
I should look here (it's initrd what loads vmlinuz, right?)?

